    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="0838" n="744" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg744"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Springer to Mr.
                            Rockhill.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-11-26"><hi rendition="#italic">November 26,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 234.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to confirm the following
                    telegram, transmitted this morning.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d693-f1"/>.</p>
                <p>I understand that these preliminary proceedings are intended as investigatory,
                    the case being in “sumario” (the nearest equivalent of which is taking
                    declarations for a grand-jury indictment). But in the case of Sanguily, the
                    United States declined to recognize the validity of the military jurisdiction in
                    preliminary or at any stage of the proceedings.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph A.
                    Springer.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
